WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.140
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday. So, recently nerdfighters have been asking me a lot about this

00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:06.000
Don't Forget to be Awesome rug available at Urban Outfitters,

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.860
like, did we license this? No. Are they paying us? No.

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:11.480
Are we happy about it? Hmm, not really.

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:13.300
Can we stop them? Yes.

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:15.120
But we're not going to.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:17.320
Welcome to the wild world of trademarks.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:20.360
So first off, Hank, Urban Outfitters is not alone in this co-opting

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:24.520
of DFTBA. Like, there's this Kate Spade wallet that says Don't Forget to Be Awesome.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:29.260
As does this Hobby Lobby poster and this dELiA*s T-shirt and this Aeropostale ad.

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:32.840
There's also this thing from Pier 1, stuff from Ron Jon Surf Shop.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:36.900
Now Hank, I know that we did not invent the phrase "Don't Forget to be Awesome." So far as I know, it was

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:39.320
invented by my friend Katie Else in 2007.

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:44.260
However, we did popularize it. The phrase had almost no Google hits or searches before nerdfighters

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:44.980
began using it.

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:49.080
And Hank, because the phrase is so closely associated with us, it would easy enough for us to go and hire

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:53.640
a lawyer and trademark it and then we could send Urban Outfitters a cease-and-desist letter that would be

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:59.540
like, "Hi, either stop selling this doormat or else publicly apologize for firing all of your Swedish employees

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:00.840
when they attempted to unionize."

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:05.720
And I would kind of like to do that, partly because I don't want corporations co-opting Nerdfighteria,

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:10.080
and partly because I am tired of getting emails asking me why, for instance, nerdfighter slogans are

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:15.400
available at Hobby Lobby, a corporation that until, like, a month ago, refused to carry menorahs.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:21.320
Ah, but wait. Here is the problem with trademarks. You cannot selectively enforce them.

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:25.860
So Hank, if we trademarked DFTBA and "Don't Forget to be Awesome," we can't just tell Urban Outfitters

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.940
and Hobby Lobby to stop making "Don't Forget to be Awesome" stuff.

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:34.060
We have to tell everyone to stop using that phrase, including, like, nerdfighters who make awesome stuff

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:35.020
and sell it on Etsy.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:38.580
Like, I'd have to send cease-and-desist letters to the people who made this amazing spoon

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:43.320
or these Hanklerfish greeting cards or this DFTBA scarf. God, that is awesome.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:46.720
And I don't want to do that because I don't want that stuff to stop existing. In fact, in many cases,

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:51.400
I want to personally buy it. But if we selectively enforce our trademark, then Urban Outfitters and Hobby Lobby

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:55.820
can be like, "No, no, no, no, no, this isn't really trademarked because look, they don't care when those

00:01:55.820 --> 00:02:01.820
people use the phrase." So the only way to stop Urban Outfitters from selling DFTBA rugs is to also stop

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:07.300
nerdfighters from selling DFTBA scarves, and of course, that's bananas. You couldn't explain this to any court.

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:11.220
But the truth is, the phrase "Don't Forget to be Awesome" doesn't belong to you and me, Hank.

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:15.220
It belongs to Nerdfighteria. So Hank, we aren't going to trademark "Don't Forget to be Awesome"

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:18.560
because it would mean claiming ownership over something that isn't really ours.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:22.120
And that means that occasionally, we're going to have to deal with companies trying to co-opt the sayings

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:23.360
of the nerdfighter community.

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:27.560
But Hank, I suggest the vast majority of nerdfighters will continue to make their own DFTBA stuff, or else

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:32.780
buy from independent creators, or buy it at DFTBA.com, where the royalties from nerdfighter merchandise

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:36.020
are always shared with the nerdfighters who design that stuff.

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:39.040
Anyway, I want to say one more thing about trademarks, because it totally fascinates me.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:44.000
If you had a lot of other people use your trademark and it becomes weakened, it can eventually become what's called,

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:50.200
"genericized", and that is every brand's nightmare. Like, aspirin used to be trademarked by the company that

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:55.140
distributed it, Bayer. And that meant that only Bayer could call their acytlsalicytic acid aspirin the way

00:02:55.140 --> 00:03:00.800
that only Advil can call their ibuprofen Advil. But because the aspirin trademark weakened over time and everyone

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:05.960
just started calling that drug aspirin, now anyone can call their aspirin "aspirin."

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:09.700
The same thing happened to escalator and thermos and it almost happened to some brands we know pretty well

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:15.100
today, including Nintendo, which marketed the phrase "game console", so people wouldn't call all consoles

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:19.220
Nintendos, and to Google, which is why Google has always very careful to talk about searching,

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:21.620
even with the rest of us always talk about "googling".

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:25.480
Hank, companies like Nintendo and Google are terrified of their trademarks becoming genericized because

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:30.020
those trademarks define them. But our community is lucky enough to be defined not by its name or

00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:35.220
by its mottoes, but by the values those words represent. And those values don't become less meaningful

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:37.880
when they are shared. In fact, quite the contrary.

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:39.685
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

